{-
   A borrow held in a field, and what reading it costs to write.

   Most of this already worked and is here to say so, because the part that did not was easy to
   mistake for the whole. `convert` has always known both directions - `convertBorrow` takes a
   reference to a place for `T -> 'T` and emits a load for `'T -> T` - so anywhere a type is *expected*
   a borrow is already transparent, and construction already borrows because the field's declared
   type is what the argument is converted to. There is no `&x` spelling at a construction site for
   the same reason there is no `->x` at a call: in this language the receiving end declares the
   convention and the supplying end writes the value.

   What did not work is dispatch, which is the one position where nothing expects a type. `p.a + p.b`
   binds `Num`'s variable to `'Int` and then looks for an instance of it, and nobody writes
   `instance Num('Int)`. So the signature *fits* and the call dies afterwards, which is why the
   fallback in emitCall tests for a selected instance rather than for a match.

   The fallback only fires when nothing accepts the arguments as they stand, so `borrows` below -
   which takes `'Int` and is handed one - still resolves to itself rather than to `owns`.
-}

data Pair {a: 'Int, b: 'Int}

data Held {n: Int}

instance Eq(Held):
    fn ==(lhs: Held, rhs: Held) -> Bool = lhs.n == rhs.n
    fn !=(lhs: Held, rhs: Held) -> Bool = !(lhs == rhs)

-- Two functions of the same name, one over the borrow and one over what it borrows. The borrow one
-- has to keep winning for an argument that is a borrow, or the fallback is rewriting calls that
-- were already right.
fn takes(v: 'Int) -> Int = 100

fn owns(v: Int) -> Int = 1

fn borrows(v: 'Int) -> Int = 2

fn main() -> Int:
    let &x = 7 :: Int
    let &y = 5 :: Int

    -- Construction: the field type says `&Int`, so the argument is borrowed rather than copied.
    -- Nothing at this site says so, and nothing needs to.
    let p = Pair {a: x, b: y}

    -- Reading with a type expected, which always worked.
    let written = p.a :: Int

    -- Dispatch with nothing expecting a type, which is what the fallback is for: 12.
    let summed = p.a + p.b

    -- An argument that something *does* accept as a borrow is left alone.
    let kept = borrows(p.a)

    -- And one that nothing accepts as a borrow is read through: `owns` takes an `Int`.
    let read = owns(p.a)

    -- A class instance reached through a borrow, which is the `Eq` shape `Instance.yana` has.
    let &h = Held {n: 3}
    let q = Pair {a: x, b: y}
    let same = if h == h then 1000 else 0

    -- 7 + 12 + 2 + 1 + 1000 + 100 = 1122.
    return written + summed + kept + read + same + takes(q.a)
